    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      <b>Five things</b>, stated plainly rather than smoothed over. (1) <b>The 421 clue source.</b> We can prove the code — the coloured dial reads 4 · 2 · 1 on the frame before the drawer opens, in two separate recordings — but neither recording shows the player reading a “421” anywhere in the room first, so we cannot tell you which object taught them the number. (2) <b>The oven temperature.</b> The card prints 250° and we read it twice on enlarged crops, but the ring in our main recording is nearer 350 when the bake begins and the cupcakes still come out; we do not know whether the game checks the ring at all, so the page gives you the card's number first and the recording's fallback second. (3) <b>The fuse-box rule.</b> Six coloured toggles and a cupcake photo taped inside the door is clearly a designed puzzle, but the mapping between photo and switches is not legible in either recording, so no “correct” switch pattern is printed here. (4) <b>What the coloured drawer contains.</b> Our main run takes the <b>Recipe</b> from it; the second run takes a <b>screwdriver</b> from the same drawer before anything else. Both are real, so the page describes the drawer as a container to empty rather than promising one specific item. (5) <b>The fruit.</b> Strawberry, kiwi and blueberries are collected and appear as toppings on the finished cupcakes, but no recording shows a decorating action, so we cannot say whether they are required or decorative. Everything else on this page — both codes, the recipe quantities, the oven's 35, the fuse layout, the +50 reward and the 4:54 clock — was read off the screen at least twice.
